1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,325
This is Istanbul.

2
00:00:01,325 --> 00:00:04,124
The only great city in the world that

3
00:00:04,124 --> 00:00:06,480
sits on two continents at once.

4
00:00:06,830 --> 00:00:09,127
For more than two thousand years,

5
00:00:09,127 --> 00:00:11,695
it has been a prize that empires were

6
00:00:11,695 --> 00:00:13,181
willing to fight for,

7
00:00:13,181 --> 00:00:14,330
again and again.

8
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:15,541
Persians,

9
00:00:15,541 --> 00:00:16,144
Avars,

10
00:00:16,144 --> 00:00:16,748
Arabs,

11
00:00:16,748 --> 00:00:17,781
Bulgarians,

12
00:00:17,781 --> 00:00:18,987
Rus warriors,

13
00:00:18,987 --> 00:00:22,950
crusaders and Ottomans all came to its walls.

14
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:25,193
And the reason is right here:

15
00:00:25,193 --> 00:00:26,770
a narrow strip of water,

16
00:00:26,770 --> 00:00:29,168
just a few hundred meters wide at one

17
00:00:29,168 --> 00:00:29,610
point.

18
00:00:29,960 --> 00:00:32,611
So why did every empire want this one

19
00:00:32,611 --> 00:00:33,030
city?

20
00:00:35,980 --> 00:00:37,629
By the end of this video,

21
00:00:37,629 --> 00:00:39,341
you'll see how one strait,

22
00:00:39,341 --> 00:00:41,688
one harbor and one set of walls made

23
00:00:41,688 --> 00:00:44,542
this the most valuable piece of land between

24
00:00:44,542 --> 00:00:45,620
Europe and Asia.

25
00:00:45,970 --> 00:00:46,471
Today,

26
00:00:46,471 --> 00:00:49,407
Istanbul is home to around fifteen and a

27
00:00:49,407 --> 00:00:50,911
half million people.

28
00:00:50,911 --> 00:00:53,775
It's by far the largest city in Turkey,

29
00:00:53,775 --> 00:00:56,210
and one of the largest in Europe.

30
00:00:56,560 --> 00:00:59,512
To understand why it matters so much,

31
00:00:59,512 --> 00:01:00,289
zoom out.

32
00:01:00,289 --> 00:01:03,086
North of Istanbul is the Black Sea,

33
00:01:03,086 --> 00:01:05,416
a sea almost as big as Spain,

34
00:01:05,416 --> 00:01:06,970
surrounded by land.

35
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:09,648
The Black Sea has only one way out

36
00:01:09,648 --> 00:01:10,646
to the oceans.

37
00:01:10,646 --> 00:01:13,705
And that way runs straight through the middle

38
00:01:13,705 --> 00:01:14,570
of Istanbul.

39
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:17,944
Ships leaving the Black Sea must pass through

40
00:01:17,944 --> 00:01:18,929
the Bosphorus,

41
00:01:18,929 --> 00:01:21,361
then cross the small Sea of Marmara,

42
00:01:21,361 --> 00:01:23,136
then pass a second strait,

43
00:01:23,136 --> 00:01:24,254
the Dardanelles,

44
00:01:24,254 --> 00:01:27,540
before reaching the Aegean and the Mediterranean.

45
00:01:27,890 --> 00:01:30,947
The Bosphorus is about thirty kilometers long.

46
00:01:30,947 --> 00:01:32,117
At its narrowest,

47
00:01:32,117 --> 00:01:34,979
it's only around seven hundred meters wide.

48
00:01:34,979 --> 00:01:37,190
You could almost shout across it.

49
00:01:37,540 --> 00:01:40,239
Whoever holds this city holds the gate.

50
00:01:40,239 --> 00:01:41,926
They can let ships pass,

51
00:01:41,926 --> 00:01:43,613
charge them for passing,

52
00:01:43,613 --> 00:01:45,300
or stop them completely.

53
00:01:45,650 --> 00:01:48,331
And the Bosphorus isn't only a sea route.

54
00:01:48,331 --> 00:01:51,586
It's also the shortest crossing between Europe and

55
00:01:51,586 --> 00:01:51,969
Asia.

56
00:01:51,969 --> 00:01:55,160
Armies and traders traveling by land have crossed

57
00:01:55,160 --> 00:01:56,820
here since ancient times.

58
00:01:57,170 --> 00:01:59,579
So the city sits on a crossroads.

59
00:01:59,579 --> 00:02:01,421
Ships go north and south,

60
00:02:01,421 --> 00:02:04,609
between the Black Sea and the Mediterranean.

61
00:02:04,609 --> 00:02:07,089
People and goods go east and west,

62
00:02:07,089 --> 00:02:08,860
between Europe and Asia.

63
00:02:09,210 --> 00:02:11,194
Then there's the Golden Horn,

64
00:02:11,194 --> 00:02:11,723
a long,

65
00:02:11,723 --> 00:02:14,235
sheltered inlet on the European side.

66
00:02:14,235 --> 00:02:16,880
It's one of the best natural harbors in

67
00:02:16,880 --> 00:02:18,930
the whole Mediterranean world.

68
00:02:19,280 --> 00:02:22,366
Add a peninsula with water on three sides,

69
00:02:22,366 --> 00:02:24,663
and you get a city that is easy

70
00:02:24,663 --> 00:02:27,246
to supply by sea and hard to attack

71
00:02:27,246 --> 00:02:27,892
by land.

72
00:02:27,892 --> 00:02:29,256
Perfect for trade,

73
00:02:29,256 --> 00:02:31,050
and perfect for defense.

74
00:02:31,400 --> 00:02:33,432
Even the currents help the city.

75
00:02:33,432 --> 00:02:36,202
Huge rivers like the Danube pour fresh water

76
00:02:36,202 --> 00:02:37,433
into the Black Sea,

77
00:02:37,433 --> 00:02:40,265
so a strong surface current flows out through

78
00:02:40,265 --> 00:02:41,188
the Bosphorus.

79
00:02:41,188 --> 00:02:44,020
Ships sailing north have to fight against it.

80
00:02:44,370 --> 00:02:47,268
So who first noticed what a perfect place

81
00:02:47,268 --> 00:02:47,957
this was?

82
00:02:47,957 --> 00:02:50,510
The answer involves a famous insult.

83
00:02:53,460 --> 00:02:55,400
In the seventh century BC,

84
00:02:55,400 --> 00:02:58,776
Greek colonists from the city of Megara sailed

85
00:02:58,776 --> 00:02:59,207
here.

86
00:02:59,207 --> 00:03:01,362
First they founded Chalcedon,

87
00:03:01,362 --> 00:03:02,799
on the Asian shore.

88
00:03:02,799 --> 00:03:04,092
Some years later,

89
00:03:04,092 --> 00:03:05,960
others founded Byzantium,

90
00:03:05,960 --> 00:03:07,540
on the European side.

91
00:03:07,890 --> 00:03:09,720
Look at the two sites.

92
00:03:09,720 --> 00:03:12,186
Chalcedon had no great harbor.

93
00:03:12,186 --> 00:03:14,652
Byzantium had the Golden Horn,

94
00:03:14,652 --> 00:03:17,834
and a peninsula that was much easier to

95
00:03:17,834 --> 00:03:18,470
defend.

96
00:03:18,820 --> 00:03:21,491
According to the Greek historian Herodotus,

97
00:03:21,491 --> 00:03:24,101
a Persian general later said the people of

98
00:03:24,101 --> 00:03:26,044
Chalcedon must have been blind,

99
00:03:26,044 --> 00:03:28,533
to settle there when the better site was

100
00:03:28,533 --> 00:03:29,990
right in front of them.

101
00:03:30,340 --> 00:03:32,620
Byzantium grew rich from the strait.

102
00:03:32,620 --> 00:03:33,729
In ancient times,

103
00:03:33,729 --> 00:03:36,440
huge amounts of grain from the lands around

104
00:03:36,440 --> 00:03:39,274
the Black Sea passed through the Bosphorus on

105
00:03:39,274 --> 00:03:41,430
their way to the cities of Greece.

106
00:03:41,780 --> 00:03:42,363
Athens,

107
00:03:42,363 --> 00:03:43,457
in particular,

108
00:03:43,457 --> 00:03:45,207
depended on that grain.

109
00:03:45,207 --> 00:03:48,779
Whoever controlled the strait could feed Athens,

110
00:03:48,779 --> 00:03:49,800
or starve it.

111
00:03:50,150 --> 00:03:51,548
So for centuries,

112
00:03:51,548 --> 00:03:54,577
Byzantium was fought over by Persians,

113
00:03:54,577 --> 00:03:55,432
Athenians,

114
00:03:55,432 --> 00:03:57,451
Spartans and Macedonians.

115
00:03:57,451 --> 00:03:59,626
Even as a small Greek town,

116
00:03:59,626 --> 00:04:01,490
it was already a prize.

117
00:04:01,840 --> 00:04:02,702
In 340 BC,

118
00:04:02,702 --> 00:04:04,192
Philip of Macedon,

119
00:04:04,192 --> 00:04:06,936
the father of Alexander the Great,

120
00:04:06,936 --> 00:04:08,503
besieged Byzantium.

121
00:04:08,503 --> 00:04:10,620
Even he failed to take it.

122
00:04:10,970 --> 00:04:13,421
But its real rise came when a Roman

123
00:04:13,421 --> 00:04:16,348
emperor decided that this was the place to

124
00:04:16,348 --> 00:04:18,730
build the new center of the world.

125
00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:23,940
By the early three hundreds AD,

126
00:04:23,940 --> 00:04:27,047
the Roman Empire's main threats were in the

127
00:04:27,047 --> 00:04:28,954
east and along the Danube.

128
00:04:28,954 --> 00:04:31,496
The city of Rome was simply too far

129
00:04:31,496 --> 00:04:31,920
away.

130
00:04:32,270 --> 00:04:36,275
So the emperor Constantine rebuilt Byzantium as his

131
00:04:36,275 --> 00:04:37,277
new capital.

132
00:04:37,277 --> 00:04:38,586
In the year 330,

133
00:04:38,586 --> 00:04:40,897
it was dedicated as New Rome.

134
00:04:40,897 --> 00:04:43,824
People soon called it Constantinople,

135
00:04:43,824 --> 00:04:45,750
the city of Constantine.

136
00:04:46,100 --> 00:04:48,709
When the western half of the Roman Empire

137
00:04:48,709 --> 00:04:50,697
collapsed in the four hundreds,

138
00:04:50,697 --> 00:04:52,374
the eastern half survived,

139
00:04:52,374 --> 00:04:54,051
ruled from Constantinople.

140
00:04:54,051 --> 00:04:56,598
Historians call it the Byzantine Empire,

141
00:04:56,598 --> 00:04:59,580
but its people simply called themselves Romans.

142
00:04:59,930 --> 00:05:01,302
To protect the city,

143
00:05:01,302 --> 00:05:03,130
in the early four hundreds,

144
00:05:03,130 --> 00:05:06,135
the emperor Theodosius the Second built a new

145
00:05:06,135 --> 00:05:08,486
line of walls across the peninsula,

146
00:05:08,486 --> 00:05:10,968
from the Sea of Marmara to the Golden

147
00:05:10,968 --> 00:05:11,360
Horn.

148
00:05:11,710 --> 00:05:13,017
On the land side,

149
00:05:13,017 --> 00:05:16,067
there was only one short front to defend,

150
00:05:16,067 --> 00:05:18,028
about six kilometers long.

151
00:05:18,028 --> 00:05:19,553
On every other side,

152
00:05:19,553 --> 00:05:21,950
the city was protected by water.

153
00:05:22,300 --> 00:05:23,935
And it wasn't one wall.

154
00:05:23,935 --> 00:05:26,320
Attackers first faced a wide moat,

155
00:05:26,320 --> 00:05:27,683
then an outer wall,

156
00:05:27,683 --> 00:05:30,340
then a taller inner wall with nearly a

157
00:05:30,340 --> 00:05:31,430
hundred towers.

158
00:05:31,780 --> 00:05:34,774
Those walls would protect the city for about

159
00:05:34,774 --> 00:05:35,971
a thousand years.

160
00:05:35,971 --> 00:05:39,963
Many historians consider them the most successful defensive

161
00:05:39,963 --> 00:05:41,160
walls ever built.

162
00:05:41,510 --> 00:05:42,409
Behind them,

163
00:05:42,409 --> 00:05:46,144
Constantinople became the largest and richest city in

164
00:05:46,144 --> 00:05:46,697
Europe.

165
00:05:46,697 --> 00:05:48,219
In the five hundreds,

166
00:05:48,219 --> 00:05:50,847
it may have had around half a million

167
00:05:50,847 --> 00:05:51,400
people.

168
00:05:51,750 --> 00:05:52,751
Its gold coin,

169
00:05:52,751 --> 00:05:53,618
the solidus,

170
00:05:53,618 --> 00:05:56,486
was trusted from Western Europe to Persia,

171
00:05:56,486 --> 00:05:59,421
and stayed remarkably stable for centuries.

172
00:05:59,421 --> 00:06:02,223
Some historians call it the dollar of the

173
00:06:02,223 --> 00:06:03,090
Middle Ages.

174
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:07,276
Crowds of tens of thousands watched chariot races

175
00:06:07,276 --> 00:06:08,733
in the Hippodrome.

176
00:06:08,733 --> 00:06:09,347
In 532,

177
00:06:09,347 --> 00:06:13,412
riots between rival racing fans almost overthrew the

178
00:06:13,412 --> 00:06:14,870
emperor Justinian.

179
00:06:15,220 --> 00:06:15,854
In 537,

180
00:06:15,854 --> 00:06:19,497
the emperor Justinian completed Hagia Sophia.

181
00:06:19,497 --> 00:06:21,794
For nearly a thousand years,

182
00:06:21,794 --> 00:06:25,200
it was the largest cathedral in the world.

183
00:06:25,550 --> 00:06:26,550
A huge city,

184
00:06:26,550 --> 00:06:30,242
an enormous treasury and control of the busiest

185
00:06:30,242 --> 00:06:32,165
crossroads in the world.

186
00:06:32,165 --> 00:06:35,473
Now every enemy of the empire knew exactly

187
00:06:35,473 --> 00:06:36,550
where to aim.

188
00:06:39,500 --> 00:06:40,097
In 626,

189
00:06:40,097 --> 00:06:43,156
the city was attacked from both sides at

190
00:06:43,156 --> 00:06:43,603
once.

191
00:06:43,603 --> 00:06:46,513
The Avars came from the European side,

192
00:06:46,513 --> 00:06:50,169
while a Persian army waited across the Bosphorus

193
00:06:50,169 --> 00:06:50,840
in Asia.

194
00:06:51,190 --> 00:06:53,642
But the two armies needed to join up.

195
00:06:53,642 --> 00:06:57,126
And the Byzantine fleet controlled the strait between

196
00:06:57,126 --> 00:06:57,514
them.

197
00:06:57,514 --> 00:06:59,320
The Persians never crossed,

198
00:06:59,320 --> 00:07:00,740
and the siege failed.

199
00:07:01,090 --> 00:07:02,809
A few decades later,

200
00:07:02,809 --> 00:07:06,576
the armies of the new Arab caliphate arrived.

201
00:07:06,576 --> 00:07:09,524
They attacked the city in the 670s,

202
00:07:09,524 --> 00:07:10,997
and again in 717,

203
00:07:10,997 --> 00:07:13,290
with a huge army and fleet.

204
00:07:13,640 --> 00:07:16,423
The Byzantines had a secret weapon:

205
00:07:16,423 --> 00:07:17,350
Greek fire,

206
00:07:17,350 --> 00:07:20,596
a liquid that kept burning even on water.

207
00:07:20,596 --> 00:07:23,147
It was sprayed onto enemy ships,

208
00:07:23,147 --> 00:07:26,239
and its exact recipe is still a mystery

209
00:07:26,239 --> 00:07:26,780
today.

210
00:07:27,130 --> 00:07:30,245
The great siege of 717 lasted about a

211
00:07:30,245 --> 00:07:30,737
year.

212
00:07:30,737 --> 00:07:32,048
A harsh winter,

213
00:07:32,048 --> 00:07:32,786
disease,

214
00:07:32,786 --> 00:07:36,147
and attacks on its fleet forced the Arab

215
00:07:36,147 --> 00:07:37,540
army to retreat.

216
00:07:37,890 --> 00:07:38,350
Then,

217
00:07:38,350 --> 00:07:38,963
in 860,

218
00:07:38,963 --> 00:07:42,030
a fleet of Rus warriors sailed down the

219
00:07:42,030 --> 00:07:45,557
rivers of Eastern Europe and across the Black

220
00:07:45,557 --> 00:07:48,010
Sea to raid the city's suburbs.

221
00:07:48,010 --> 00:07:50,770
The Rus would return several times.

222
00:07:51,120 --> 00:07:54,904
Bulgarian rulers marched to the walls more than

223
00:07:54,904 --> 00:07:56,007
once as well.

224
00:07:56,007 --> 00:07:57,347
Again and again,

225
00:07:57,347 --> 00:07:58,845
the city survived.

226
00:07:58,845 --> 00:08:00,106
The walls held,

227
00:08:00,106 --> 00:08:02,550
and food kept arriving by sea.

228
00:08:02,900 --> 00:08:04,338
That was the secret.

229
00:08:04,338 --> 00:08:05,981
To take Constantinople,

230
00:08:05,981 --> 00:08:08,515
an enemy had to break the land walls

231
00:08:08,515 --> 00:08:11,117
and control the sea at the same time.

232
00:08:11,117 --> 00:08:12,144
For centuries,

233
00:08:12,144 --> 00:08:13,650
no one could do both.

234
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:16,322
The first foreign army to break in didn't

235
00:08:16,322 --> 00:08:17,814
come from the east at all.

236
00:08:17,814 --> 00:08:19,418
It came from Western Europe,

237
00:08:19,418 --> 00:08:21,242
and it was supposed to be on the

238
00:08:21,242 --> 00:08:21,850
same side.

239
00:08:22,200 --> 00:08:22,821
In 1204,

240
00:08:22,821 --> 00:08:24,201
the Fourth Crusade,

241
00:08:24,201 --> 00:08:26,546
which had set out to fight in the

242
00:08:26,546 --> 00:08:27,305
Holy Land,

243
00:08:27,305 --> 00:08:29,996
turned against Constantinople instead.

244
00:08:29,996 --> 00:08:33,031
The crusaders broke in from the Golden Horn

245
00:08:33,031 --> 00:08:34,480
and sacked the city.

246
00:08:34,830 --> 00:08:35,366
Venice,

247
00:08:35,366 --> 00:08:38,116
which had supplied the crusaders' ships,

248
00:08:38,116 --> 00:08:40,597
took a large share of the treasures.

249
00:08:40,597 --> 00:08:43,481
The famous bronze horses that stand in St.

250
00:08:43,481 --> 00:08:47,370
Mark's Basilica in Venice were taken from Constantinople.

251
00:08:47,720 --> 00:08:50,608
Italian merchants moved in for good.

252
00:08:50,608 --> 00:08:53,965
Genoa ran its own walled trading colony at

253
00:08:53,965 --> 00:08:54,589
Galata,

254
00:08:54,589 --> 00:08:56,463
across the Golden Horn.

255
00:08:56,463 --> 00:08:57,868
Its Galata Tower,

256
00:08:57,868 --> 00:08:59,039
built in 1348,

257
00:08:59,039 --> 00:09:00,600
still stands today.

258
00:09:00,950 --> 00:09:03,930
The Byzantines retook their city in 1261.

259
00:09:03,930 --> 00:09:06,698
But it never recovered its old wealth.

260
00:09:06,698 --> 00:09:09,394
And a new power was rising right next

261
00:09:09,394 --> 00:09:09,820
door.

262
00:09:12,770 --> 00:09:13,862
By the 1400s,

263
00:09:13,862 --> 00:09:18,308
the Ottoman Turks had conquered almost everything around

264
00:09:18,308 --> 00:09:19,088
the city,

265
00:09:19,088 --> 00:09:20,882
in Asia and in Europe.

266
00:09:20,882 --> 00:09:25,094
Constantinople was an island of Roman rule surrounded

267
00:09:25,094 --> 00:09:26,420
by Ottoman land.

268
00:09:26,770 --> 00:09:29,025
Its population had collapsed too.

269
00:09:29,025 --> 00:09:31,811
From perhaps half a million people at its

270
00:09:31,811 --> 00:09:32,341
height,

271
00:09:32,341 --> 00:09:34,530
it now had maybe fifty thousand.

272
00:09:34,880 --> 00:09:35,500
In 1453,

273
00:09:35,500 --> 00:09:37,982
the young sultan Mehmed the Second,

274
00:09:37,982 --> 00:09:39,843
only twenty-one years old,

275
00:09:39,843 --> 00:09:41,221
decided to take it.

276
00:09:41,221 --> 00:09:43,909
He had already built a fortress on the

277
00:09:43,909 --> 00:09:46,735
Bosphorus to cut off help from the Black

278
00:09:46,735 --> 00:09:47,080
Sea.

279
00:09:47,430 --> 00:09:50,158
His army was many times larger than the

280
00:09:50,158 --> 00:09:50,909
defenders,

281
00:09:50,909 --> 00:09:54,046
who numbered perhaps seven or eight thousand.

282
00:09:54,046 --> 00:09:56,092
And he brought something new:

283
00:09:56,092 --> 00:09:57,320
enormous cannons.

284
00:09:57,670 --> 00:10:00,041
One of them was built by a Hungarian

285
00:10:00,041 --> 00:10:01,515
engineer called Orban.

286
00:10:01,515 --> 00:10:04,526
It could fire stone balls weighing hundreds of

287
00:10:04,526 --> 00:10:05,231
kilograms,

288
00:10:05,231 --> 00:10:07,474
and it took dozens of oxen to move

289
00:10:07,474 --> 00:10:07,730
it.

290
00:10:08,080 --> 00:10:10,807
The defenders had blocked the entrance to the

291
00:10:10,807 --> 00:10:13,594
Golden Horn with a huge chain stretched across

292
00:10:13,594 --> 00:10:14,246
the water.

293
00:10:14,246 --> 00:10:16,440
So Mehmed did something astonishing.

294
00:10:16,790 --> 00:10:19,619
He had dozens of ships dragged overland,

295
00:10:19,619 --> 00:10:21,828
across the hills behind Galata,

296
00:10:21,828 --> 00:10:24,588
and launched them into the Golden Horn,

297
00:10:24,588 --> 00:10:25,830
behind the chain.

298
00:10:26,180 --> 00:10:30,116
After about fifty-three days of siege and bombardment,

299
00:10:30,116 --> 00:10:32,119
on the twenty-ninth of May,

300
00:10:32,119 --> 00:10:32,548
1453,

301
00:10:32,548 --> 00:10:35,554
the Ottoman army broke through the walls.

302
00:10:35,554 --> 00:10:39,060
The last Byzantine emperor died in the fighting.

303
00:10:39,410 --> 00:10:41,599
After almost eleven hundred years,

304
00:10:41,599 --> 00:10:44,664
the Roman Empire that Constantine had moved here

305
00:10:44,664 --> 00:10:45,290
was gone.

306
00:10:45,640 --> 00:10:48,874
Many historians use 1453 to mark the end

307
00:10:48,874 --> 00:10:50,451
of the Middle Ages.

308
00:10:50,451 --> 00:10:54,315
Greek scholars who fled to Italy carried ancient

309
00:10:54,315 --> 00:10:55,656
texts with them,

310
00:10:55,656 --> 00:10:58,180
adding fuel to the Renaissance.

311
00:10:58,530 --> 00:11:00,994
Mehmed made the city his capital.

312
00:11:00,994 --> 00:11:03,169
Hagia Sophia became a mosque,

313
00:11:03,169 --> 00:11:06,213
and the city was rebuilt and filled again

314
00:11:06,213 --> 00:11:09,620
with people brought in from across the empire.

315
00:11:09,970 --> 00:11:11,197
For the Ottomans,

316
00:11:11,197 --> 00:11:13,651
the city was the key to everything.

317
00:11:13,651 --> 00:11:14,401
From here,

318
00:11:14,401 --> 00:11:17,536
they could rule both their European and Asian

319
00:11:17,536 --> 00:11:18,014
lands,

320
00:11:18,014 --> 00:11:20,468
and control all trade in and out of

321
00:11:20,468 --> 00:11:21,490
the Black Sea.

322
00:11:21,840 --> 00:11:23,366
If you're enjoying this,

323
00:11:23,366 --> 00:11:24,037
subscribe.

324
00:11:24,037 --> 00:11:26,417
Every week we find the story hidden in

325
00:11:26,417 --> 00:11:27,210
another map.

326
00:11:30,160 --> 00:11:31,624
Under the Ottomans,

327
00:11:31,624 --> 00:11:33,161
the city grew again.

328
00:11:33,161 --> 00:11:34,186
In the 1500s,

329
00:11:34,186 --> 00:11:37,406
it was probably the largest city in Europe,

330
00:11:37,406 --> 00:11:38,943
filled with mosques,

331
00:11:38,943 --> 00:11:40,480
markets and palaces.

332
00:11:40,830 --> 00:11:44,257
The great architect Mimar Sinan built the Süleymaniye

333
00:11:44,257 --> 00:11:46,414
Mosque in the 1550s on one of the

334
00:11:46,414 --> 00:11:47,303
city's hills,

335
00:11:47,303 --> 00:11:50,285
and the sultans ruled from the Topkapı Palace,

336
00:11:50,285 --> 00:11:53,395
overlooking the meeting of the Bosphorus and the

337
00:11:53,395 --> 00:11:54,220
Golden Horn.

338
00:11:54,570 --> 00:11:57,050
But as the Ottoman Empire weakened,

339
00:11:57,050 --> 00:11:59,737
a new power looked at the straits with

340
00:11:59,737 --> 00:12:00,839
great interest.

341
00:12:00,839 --> 00:12:01,390
Russia.

342
00:12:01,740 --> 00:12:04,734
Russia's southern ports were on the Black Sea.

343
00:12:04,734 --> 00:12:07,856
Every Russian warship and grain ship heading for

344
00:12:07,856 --> 00:12:10,914
the Mediterranean had to pass through Istanbul,

345
00:12:10,914 --> 00:12:13,080
under the guns of another empire.

346
00:12:13,430 --> 00:12:16,779
Some Russian thinkers even called the city Tsargrad,

347
00:12:16,779 --> 00:12:18,358
the city of the emperor,

348
00:12:18,358 --> 00:12:21,075
and dreamed of taking it back for Orthodox

349
00:12:21,075 --> 00:12:21,960
Christianity.

350
00:12:22,310 --> 00:12:24,578
Britain and France saw the danger.

351
00:12:24,578 --> 00:12:26,781
If Russia controlled the straits,

352
00:12:26,781 --> 00:12:30,151
its fleet could sail freely into the Mediterranean,

353
00:12:30,151 --> 00:12:32,031
close to their trade routes.

354
00:12:32,031 --> 00:12:34,752
So they often backed the Ottomans to keep

355
00:12:34,752 --> 00:12:35,530
Russia out.

356
00:12:35,880 --> 00:12:38,256
That rivalry was one of the causes of

357
00:12:38,256 --> 00:12:40,132
the Crimean War in the 1850s.

358
00:12:40,132 --> 00:12:41,883
And in the First World War,

359
00:12:41,883 --> 00:12:44,259
the straits were at the center of the

360
00:12:44,259 --> 00:12:45,260
fighting again.

361
00:12:45,610 --> 00:12:46,240
In 1915,

362
00:12:46,240 --> 00:12:49,530
British and French forces tried to force their

363
00:12:49,530 --> 00:12:52,890
way through the Dardanelles and reach Istanbul.

364
00:12:52,890 --> 00:12:54,640
The naval attack failed,

365
00:12:54,640 --> 00:12:57,720
and the landings at Gallipoli turned into a

366
00:12:57,720 --> 00:12:58,140
long,

367
00:12:58,140 --> 00:13:01,220
costly campaign before the Allies withdrew.

368
00:13:01,570 --> 00:13:02,374
In secret,

369
00:13:02,374 --> 00:13:05,663
the Allies had even agreed that Russia would

370
00:13:05,663 --> 00:13:07,709
get the city after the war.

371
00:13:07,709 --> 00:13:11,290
But Russia's revolution in 1917 ended that plan.

372
00:13:11,640 --> 00:13:12,825
After the war,

373
00:13:12,825 --> 00:13:16,301
Allied troops occupied Istanbul until 1923.

374
00:13:16,301 --> 00:13:19,855
When the Republic of Turkey was founded that

375
00:13:19,855 --> 00:13:20,329
year,

376
00:13:20,329 --> 00:13:22,462
its capital became Ankara,

377
00:13:22,462 --> 00:13:23,410
far inland.

378
00:13:23,760 --> 00:13:26,239
Part of the reason was the lesson of

379
00:13:26,239 --> 00:13:26,842
history.

380
00:13:26,842 --> 00:13:29,120
A capital on the strait was rich,

381
00:13:29,120 --> 00:13:31,733
but it was also exposed to every fleet

382
00:13:31,733 --> 00:13:33,140
that could reach it.

383
00:13:33,490 --> 00:13:34,183
In 1936,

384
00:13:34,183 --> 00:13:37,722
the Montreux Convention set the rules for the

385
00:13:37,722 --> 00:13:38,415
straits.

386
00:13:38,415 --> 00:13:40,108
Turkey controls them.

387
00:13:40,108 --> 00:13:43,571
Merchant ships can pass freely in peacetime,

388
00:13:43,571 --> 00:13:46,110
but warships face strict limits.

389
00:13:46,460 --> 00:13:48,929
That treaty is still in force today.

390
00:13:48,929 --> 00:13:51,265
And in times of war in the region,

391
00:13:51,265 --> 00:13:54,202
it has made Turkey's control of the straits

392
00:13:54,202 --> 00:13:55,670
as important as ever.

393
00:13:58,620 --> 00:13:59,103
Today,

394
00:13:59,103 --> 00:14:02,136
tens of thousands of ships pass through the

395
00:14:02,136 --> 00:14:03,653
Bosphorus every year,

396
00:14:03,653 --> 00:14:06,411
many of them tankers and bulk carriers,

397
00:14:06,411 --> 00:14:09,790
sailing right past the city's homes and mosques.

398
00:14:10,140 --> 00:14:12,237
Grain from Ukraine and Russia,

399
00:14:12,237 --> 00:14:14,604
and oil from the Black Sea region,

400
00:14:14,604 --> 00:14:16,296
still travel this route,

401
00:14:16,296 --> 00:14:18,528
just as grain did in the days of

402
00:14:18,528 --> 00:14:19,610
ancient Athens.

403
00:14:19,960 --> 00:14:23,116
And the old land bridge is busier than

404
00:14:23,116 --> 00:14:23,602
ever.

405
00:14:23,602 --> 00:14:27,486
The first bridge across the Bosphorus opened in

406
00:14:27,486 --> 00:14:27,972
1973.

407
00:14:27,972 --> 00:14:30,481
Today there are three bridges,

408
00:14:30,481 --> 00:14:32,908
a rail tunnel opened in 2013,

409
00:14:32,908 --> 00:14:35,660
and a road tunnel opened in 2016.

410
00:14:36,010 --> 00:14:36,780
Every day,

411
00:14:36,780 --> 00:14:40,280
huge numbers of people commute between Europe and

412
00:14:40,280 --> 00:14:40,700
Asia.

413
00:14:40,700 --> 00:14:41,400
For them,

414
00:14:41,400 --> 00:14:44,620
changing continents is just the trip to work.

415
00:14:44,970 --> 00:14:47,277
The city has grown enormously.

416
00:14:47,277 --> 00:14:50,699
Around one million people lived here in 1950.

417
00:14:50,699 --> 00:14:53,750
Today it's more than fifteen times that.

418
00:14:54,100 --> 00:14:56,885
Even its name tells the story of a

419
00:14:56,885 --> 00:14:57,840
crossroads.

420
00:14:57,840 --> 00:15:01,580
Istanbul most likely comes from a Greek phrase

421
00:15:01,580 --> 00:15:03,251
meaning to the city.

422
00:15:03,251 --> 00:15:06,673
It became the city's only official name in

423
00:15:06,673 --> 00:15:07,150
1930.

424
00:15:07,500 --> 00:15:10,226
There's one danger the walls can't stop.

425
00:15:10,226 --> 00:15:13,417
Istanbul lies close to a major earthquake fault

426
00:15:13,417 --> 00:15:15,146
under the Sea of Marmara,

427
00:15:15,146 --> 00:15:17,939
and the city is working to strengthen its

428
00:15:17,939 --> 00:15:18,670
buildings.

429
00:15:19,020 --> 00:15:21,980
But the geography that made the city great

430
00:15:21,980 --> 00:15:24,597
hasn't changed in thousands of years.

431
00:15:24,597 --> 00:15:26,180
And it isn't going to.

432
00:15:26,530 --> 00:15:28,740
So let's put the whole story together.

433
00:15:29,090 --> 00:15:32,153
Istanbul controls the only sea route between the

434
00:15:32,153 --> 00:15:34,216
Black Sea and the Mediterranean,

435
00:15:34,216 --> 00:15:37,404
and the shortest crossing between Europe and Asia.

436
00:15:37,404 --> 00:15:39,842
It has one of the best natural harbors

437
00:15:39,842 --> 00:15:40,780
in the region.

438
00:15:41,130 --> 00:15:42,375
That made it rich,

439
00:15:42,375 --> 00:15:44,145
and that made it a target.

440
00:15:44,145 --> 00:15:47,028
Its walls and its fleets held off attackers

441
00:15:47,028 --> 00:15:48,470
for a thousand years,

442
00:15:48,470 --> 00:15:51,747
until cannons and ships dragged over land finally

443
00:15:51,747 --> 00:15:52,730
broke through.

444
00:15:53,080 --> 00:15:56,399
That's why Istanbul was the prize every empire

445
00:15:56,399 --> 00:15:56,964
wanted.

446
00:15:56,964 --> 00:15:58,730
Not because of its gold,

447
00:15:58,730 --> 00:15:59,860
or its palaces,

448
00:15:59,860 --> 00:16:02,120
but because of where it stands:

449
00:16:02,120 --> 00:16:04,874
on a narrow strip of water between two

450
00:16:04,874 --> 00:16:06,640
seas and two continents.

451
00:16:06,990 --> 00:16:11,276
Every map hides a story like this one.

452
00:16:11,276 --> 00:16:15,453
If you want to discover the next one,

453
00:16:15,453 --> 00:16:18,640
subscribe to Timeline Earth.
